  #sol-item(1, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The gain belongs to the partners who owned the assets while it arose.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The incoming partner has not contributed to the gain, so he does not share it.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    260,000 − 200,000 = ₦60,000 credited to the partners.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A fall in the value of an asset is a loss on revaluation, and losses are debited.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    He is paying the old partners for a share of the reputation they built.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Every partner who will enjoy the reputation bears the write-off, in the new ratio.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    300,000 + 120,000 = ₦420,000, and C's quarter is ₦105,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    One-quarter of ₦420,000 = ₦105,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    All three balances are his, and they are settled together.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    They will enjoy the reputation after he leaves, so they bear its cost.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The firm owes him the money, so it becomes a loan until it is paid.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It records the assets realised, the expenses and the proceeds.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    They are transferred at book value, and the proceeds are credited separately, so the difference is the profit or loss.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Only the assets and their proceeds go through the realisation account.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The costs of winding up come first and the owners' residual claim last.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The loan is a debt, and it ranks with the creditors ahead of capital.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A debit balance is a deficit, and the partner owes it to the firm.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The gain or loss is settled with the partners, in the old ratio.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Capitals are adjusted through the current accounts to agree with the new ratio.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    One-third of 120,000 = ₦40,000 credited to Z.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Z's ₦40,000 is borne by the two continuing partners in their new ratio, 1:1, so ₦20,000 each.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The settlement with the old partners must precede the change.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Purchased goodwill is an asset; internally generated goodwill is not carried.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The proceeds are ₦12,000 below book value, and the loss is shared by the partners.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    They are a cost of winding up, so they reduce the amount available.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The provision is no longer needed, so the release is a gain in the realisation account.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is shared among the partners in the profit-sharing ratio.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The cash is shared in the old ratio as payment for the share each has given up.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The tax rate is set by law, not by the partners.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The two-step treatment raises it to credit the old partners and then eliminates it.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is the running account between the firm and the partner.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The goods left the business for the partner, so his drawings are increased and purchases reduced.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It arose while the old ratio applied.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    500,000 − 180,000 − 60,000 = ₦260,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    His ₦80,000 buys a fifth, so the firm is valued at ₦400,000 against net assets of ₦320,000, and the goodwill is ₦80,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Proceeds are credited, not debited; the debit side carries the assets and the costs.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A credit balance is money the firm owes him, so it is added to his capital.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The settlement must reflect the true value at the moment of the change.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
